### 3. Source-level prior work on LLM Bayesian/probabilistic updating on supplied likelihood tables

Several recent papers directly test Bayesian updating in LLMs, but the exact E1 regime (explicit supplied behavior/policy table in a two-world epistemic setup, numeric posteriors against exact Bayes ground truth, narrative-vs-table presentation axis) is not fully covered.

- “Enough Coin Flips Can Make LLMs Act Bayesian” (Gupta et al., arXiv:2503.04722, ACL 2025) shows LLMs broadly follow Bayesian posterior updates in a controlled biased-coin ICL setting once sufficient evidence is provided; deviations are mainly from miscalibrated priors rather than flawed updating mechanics.
- “LLMs are not (consistently) Bayesian” (arXiv:2605.06915) quantifies an “information processing gap” between observed and Bayes-optimal updates and finds that forcing explicit Bayes’ law does not consistently improve downstream performance.
- “Bayesian Teaching Enables Probabilistic Reasoning in Large Language Models” (arXiv:2503.17523) demonstrates that LLMs initially fall short of normative Bayesian updating but can be taught to approximate it via in-context examples of a Bayesian assistant.
- “BayesBench” (arXiv:2606.30850) introduces a multi-turn evidence-accumulation benchmark that polls elicited posteriors after each piece of evidence and compares trajectories to Bayesian references.
- “The Geometry of Ignorance” (arXiv:2609.02959) identifies a geometric “direction of ignorance” in the unembedding that encodes the unigram prior and shows LLMs perform a tempered Bayesian update whose prior reliance decreases with more context.

Closest collision is the coin-flip and multi-turn evidence paradigms; none of the above use an *explicitly supplied behavior/policy table* with registered likelihood ratios in a two-world epistemic setting plus the narrative-vs-table axis. The v0 prototype already implements the core task, so E1’s contribution is best framed as a calibration/baseline study on that substrate rather than a novel family. A targeted source check of the v0 code (or a short written confirmation from the architect) would confirm whether any of the above papers’ exact setups overlap with v0’s implementation.

