## Q1 — Standalone question or baseline anchor?

**My read: as currently worded, E1 is primarily an anchor, and its standalone novelty is weak.** The core phenomenon — do probabilistic reasoners under-weight supplied likelihoods or base rates — is one of the most heavily studied patterns in behavioral decision research, and the log-odds coefficient design above is, to my recollection `[R]`, the standard estimator from that literature rather than anything new. LLM-directed replications of it are likely to exist. That is precisely what Q3's search must establish.

**However, there is a defensible standalone variant specific to your mission**, and it is the thing that makes E1 more than a calibration run:

> Does delivering the likelihoods as *an agent's disclosed behavior policy* ("Agent B sends this message in 80% of states of type w1") degrade updating relative to a formally identical abstract presentation ("a draw from this urn yields this signal 80% of the time")?

That is an interaction claim — agentic framing × evidence weighting — with the same ground truth, the same estimator, and β_LR as the outcome. It is closer to the mission's epistemic-game interest than plain Bayesian accuracy; it bears directly on whether later S-questions can assume that supplied policies are even being read as likelihoods; and it is a genuinely plausible two-sided bet. Note it is partially adjacent to L07's game-structure-versus-framing concern, which strengthens the need for the Q3 search.

Recommendation: record **both** readings on the card — E1-as-anchor (which is what later S2/S3 need) and E1-framing (the candidate standalone contribution) — and let the Q3 search decide whether the second survives. Do not merge them into one claim.

